To make the circle element look more 3D, you can apply some CSS properties that create the illusion of depth and perspective. Here's an updated version of your existing `.circle` styles with these modifications:

```css
.circle {
  fill: radial-gradient(white, lightblue);
  filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray) brightness(0.8); /* Add brightness() to reduce the intensity of the circle */
  transform-style: preserve-3d; /* Enable 3D transforms */
  transform: translateZ(5px); /* Move the circle forward by 5 pixels */
  transition: transform 0.3s ease-in-out;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc);
  box-shadow: 10px 10px 5px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75);
  border-radius: 70%;
}
```

Here's how these changes affect the circle:

- `filter: brightness(0.8)` reduces the overall brightness of the circle, which helps it appear less intense and more solid when viewed from different angles.
- `transform-style: preserve-3d` enables 3D transforms, allowing us to rotate or move the circle in 3D space.
- `transform: translateZ(5px)` moves the circle forward by 5 pixels on the z-axis, creating a sense of depth.

Feel free to adjust the values of `translateZ()` to suit your needs. You may also want to experiment with other 3D transforms such as `rotateX()`, `rotateY()`, and `rotateZ()` to achieve the desired effect. Remember to always test your design across multiple devices and browsers to ensure compatibility and accessibility.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>
