if randomise is false, then the index of the item in the array should be item.position-1
whereas if randomize is true, the item can be placed anywhere in the resultant array

response:
 Here's an implementation in TypeScript:

```typescript
type Item = { name: string; position: number; randomize?: boolean };

function shuffleArray(arr: Item[]): Item[] {
  const result: Item[] = [];

  for (let I = 0; I < arr.length; i++) {
    const item = arr[i];

    if (!item.randomize) {
      result[item.position - 1] = item;
    } else {
      result.push(item);
    }
  }

  return result;
}

const fruits: Item[] = [
  { name: "apple", position: 1, randomize: false },
  { name: "pineapple", position: 2, randomize: false },
  { name: "orange", position: 3, randomize: true },
  { name: "banana", position: 4, randomize: true },
  { name: "grapes", position: 8, randomize: false },
  { name: "lemon", position: 11, randomize: true },
  { name: "jack fruit", position: 10, randomize: false },
  { name: "dragon fruit", position: 5, randomize: false },
  { name: "green apple", position: 9, randomize: false },
];

const shuffledFruits = shuffleArray(fruits);
console.log(shuffledFruits);
```

The `shuffleArray()` function takes an array of items and returns a new array with items arranged according to their positions and randomization settings. If `randomize` is false, the item is added to the resulting array at its specified position. Otherwise, it's added randomly. The function uses a simple loop to iterate over the input array and add each item to the output array accordingly. The resulting array is then returned. You can use this function to shuffle your array based on the given criteria. In our example, we pass the `fruits` array to the `shuffleArray()` function and log the result to console. This will output the shuffled array with items arranged based on their positions and randomization settings

