In this implementation, we define a new function `sortAsc` that takes an array `arr` as its argument. Inside the function, we create a new arrow function (an anonymous function with a shorthand syntax) and pass it to the built-in `sort()` method of arrays. This arrow function is used to compare two elements `a` and `b` during the sorting process. If `a` is less than `b`, then it returns a negative number, indicating that `a` should come before `b`. If `a` is equal to `b`, then it returns zero, indicating that they should have the same position. If `a` is greater than `b`, then it returns a positive number, indicating that `b` should come before `a`. By default, `sort()` uses the Unicode code points of characters to determine their relative positions, but by providing our own comparison function, we can customize the sorting behavior according to our needs. In this case, we simply subtract `a` from `b` to get the difference between them, which will be negative if `a` is less than `b`, zero if they are equal, or positive if `a` is greater than `b`.

