- HTML (HyperText Markup Language): used to create structured documents with text, images, and links that can be displayed on the web. It's a markup language, meaning it uses tags to format content. Common tags include <h1> for headings, <p> for paragraphs, and <img> for images.
- SGML (Standard Generalized Markup Language): a more general-purpose markup language used to define document structures and relationships. HTML is a subset of SGML. SGML allows for custom tag definitions, making it more flexible but also more complex than HTML.

JavaScript:

- Variables: named containers used to store data values. Use the keyword var to declare variables, followed by a name and an assignment operator (=). Example: var myName = "John Doe";
- Functions: reusable blocks of code that perform specific tasks. Use the keyword function to declare functions, followed by a name, parentheses (), and curly braces {}. Example: function greetUser() { alert("Hello, " + myName + "!"); }
- Conditional statements: allow code to execute differently based on certain conditions. Use if/else statements to implement conditional logic. Example: if (myAge > 18) { alert("You are an adult."); } else { alert("You are not yet an adult."); }
- Loops: allow code to repeat actions multiple times. Use for loops or while loops to implement repetitive logic. Example: for (var I = 0; I < 10; i++) { alert(i); }

CSS (Cascading Style Sheets):

- Selectors: rules that match elements in an HTML document. Use selectors to apply styles to specific elements. Examples: h1 { color: red; }, .myClass { font-size: 24px; }, #myId { background-color: blue; }
- Properties: individual style attributes that can be applied to elements. Examples: color, font-size, background-color.
- Values: specific settings or options for properties. Examples: red, 24px, blue.

HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol):

- GET request: used to retrieve data from a server. The URL in the browser address bar represents a GET request. Example: http://example.

